@*
=============================================================================
BLAZOR COMPONENT - Index.razor (Main Parts List Page)
=============================================================================
This is a Blazor component - a reusable piece of UI that combines HTML markup
with C# code. Blazor uses a component-based architecture similar to React or Vue.

📖 BLAZOR FILE STRUCTURE:
- .razor files contain both markup (HTML) and code (C#)
- Top section: Directives (@page, @inject, @using)
- Middle section: HTML markup with Razor syntax
- Bottom section: @code block with C# logic

📖 RAZOR SYNTAX:
- @ prefix: Switch from HTML to C# expression
- @{ }: C# code block
- @if, @foreach: C# control flow
- @bind: Two-way data binding
- @@: Escape @ symbol in output

📚 Learn more:
- Blazor docs: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/blazor/
- Component lifecycle: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/blazor/components/lifecycle
=============================================================================
*@

@*
📖 PAGE DIRECTIVE:
- Defines the URL route for this component
- "/" means this is the home/index page
- Can have parameters: @page "/parts/{id:int}"
*@
@page "/"

@*
📖 INJECT DIRECTIVE (Dependency Injection):
- @inject tells Blazor to inject a service
- IPartsService: The interface we want
- PartsService: The variable name to use in code
- DI container provides the implementation

This is the same pattern as constructor injection in classes:
  public class MyClass
  {
      public MyClass(IPartsService partsService) { }
  }

📚 DI in Blazor: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/blazor/fundamentals/dependency-injection
*@
@inject IPartsService PartsService

@*
=============================================================================
SEARCH INPUT - Data Binding Example
=============================================================================
📖 @bind DIRECTIVE:
- Creates two-way data binding between HTML element and C# property
- @bind="searchQuery" means:
  - Value displays searchQuery
  - Changes update searchQuery

📖 @bind:event:
- Default bind event is "onchange" (fires when focus leaves)
- @bind:event="oninput" fires on every keystroke
- Better for real-time search

📖 @onkeyup:
- Event handler for keyup events
- Calls OnSearchKeyUp method with KeyboardEventArgs
- "OnSearchKeyUp" is a method in the @code block

Alternative event syntax:
  @onkeyup="HandleKeyUp"           // Method reference
  @onkeyup="() => DoSomething()"   // Lambda expression
  @onkeyup="e => HandleKey(e)"     // Lambda with parameter
*@
<div class="search-container">
    <input type="text"
           class="search-input"
           placeholder="Search parts by name, number, or manufacturer..."
           @bind="searchQuery"
           @bind:event="oninput"
           @onkeyup="OnSearchKeyUp" />
</div>

@*
=============================================================================
CARD HEADER - Razor Expressions
=============================================================================
📖 RAZOR EXPRESSIONS:
- @(...) evaluates a C# expression and outputs the result
- @(parts?.Count() ?? 0) demonstrates:
  - ?. (null-conditional): Returns null if parts is null
  - ?? (null-coalescing): Returns 0 if left side is null

📖 @onclick DIRECTIVE:
- Binds a click event to a method
- Method can be async: @onclick="async () => await DoAsync()"
*@
<div class="card-header mb-md">
    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; width: 100%;">
        <span class="text-muted">@(parts?.Count() ?? 0) parts found</span>
        <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="ShowAddPartModal">+ Add Part</button>
    </div>
</div>

@*
=============================================================================
CONDITIONAL RENDERING - @if / else if / else
=============================================================================
📖 @if DIRECTIVE:
- Conditionally includes markup in the render output
- NOT hidden with CSS - actually not rendered when false
- Better for performance when elements are complex

📖 LOADING STATE PATTERN:
- Show spinner while isLoading is true
- Show content when data is ready
- Show empty state when no data

Alternative: Use CSS [hidden] for simple show/hide
*@
@if (isLoading)
{
    <div class="loading">
        <div class="spinner"></div>
        <p>Loading parts...</p>
    </div>
}
else if (parts != null && parts.Any())
{
    @*
    =========================================================================
    LIST RENDERING - @foreach
    =========================================================================
    📖 @foreach DIRECTIVE:
    - Renders markup for each item in a collection
    - 'part' is the loop variable
    - Each iteration creates DOM elements

    📖 @onclick WITH LAMBDA:
    - @onclick="() => SelectPart(part)"
    - Lambda captures 'part' variable
    - Each card gets its own part reference

    ⚠️ CLOSURE WARNING:
    If using for loop with index, be careful:
      @for (int i = 0; i < items.Count; i++)
      {
          // WRONG: All buttons would use final i value
          <button @onclick="() => Select(i)">
      }

    SOLUTION: Use foreach or capture variable:
      var captured = i;
      <button @onclick="() => Select(captured)">
    *@
    <div class="parts-grid">
        @foreach (var part in parts)
        {
            @*
            📖 @onclick WITH ARGUMENT:
            - () => SelectPart(part) creates a lambda
            - Lambda is needed to pass parameters
            - Without lambda: @onclick="SelectPart" (no parameters)
            *@
            <div class="part-card" @onclick="() => SelectPart(part)">
                <div class="part-image-placeholder">No Image</div>
                <div class="part-info">
                    @*
                    📖 SIMPLE EXPRESSIONS:
                    - @part.PartNumber outputs the property value
                    - No parentheses needed for simple properties
                    - Automatically HTML-encoded (XSS protection)
                    *@
                    <div class="part-number">@part.PartNumber</div>
                    <div class="part-name">@part.Name</div>
                    <div class="part-meta">
                        @*
                        📖 NULL-COALESCING IN RAZOR:
                        - @(part.Category?.Name ?? "Uncategorized")
                        - Handles null Category gracefully
                        - Parentheses group the expression
                        *@
                        <span class="text-muted">@(part.Category?.Name ?? "Uncategorized")</span>
                        @*
                        📖 CALLING METHODS IN RAZOR:
                        - @GetQuantityClass(part) calls a method
                        - Return value becomes the CSS class
                        - Dynamic styling based on data
                        *@
                        <span class="part-quantity @GetQuantityClass(part)">
                            @part.Quantity in stock
                        </span>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        }
    </div>
}
else
{
    @* Empty state when no parts exist *@
    <div class="card">
        <p class="text-muted" style="text-align: center;">
            No parts found. Add your first part to get started!
        </p>
    </div>
}

@*
=============================================================================
MODAL - Add New Part Form
=============================================================================
📖 CONDITIONAL MODAL:
- @if (showAddModal) renders modal only when needed
- Modal overlay covers entire screen
- Clicking overlay closes modal

📖 @onclick:stopPropagation:
- Prevents click event from bubbling to parent
- Without it: clicking modal content would close it
- Event modifiers: @onclick:preventDefault also available

📖 EditForm COMPONENT:
- Blazor's form handling component
- Model="newPart": Object being edited
- OnValidSubmit: Handler called when form is valid
- Provides validation context

📖 InputText, InputNumber, InputSelect:
- Blazor input components
- @bind-Value: Two-way binding to model property
- Automatically update the model object
- Provide validation support

📚 Forms guide: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/blazor/forms/
*@
@if (showAddModal)
{
    <div class="modal-overlay" @onclick="CloseModals">
        @* stopPropagation prevents closing when clicking inside modal *@
        <div class="modal" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
            <div class="modal-header">
                <h3>Add New Part</h3>
                <button class="btn-icon" @onclick="CloseModals">&times;</button>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">
                @*
                📖 EditForm EXPLAINED:
                - Wraps form inputs with validation context
                - Model: The object to bind form fields to
                - OnValidSubmit: Called only if validation passes
                - OnInvalidSubmit: Called if validation fails
                *@
                <EditForm Model="newPart" OnValidSubmit="SaveNewPart">
                    <div class="form-group">
                        <label class="form-label">Part Number *</label>
                        @*
                        📖 InputText COMPONENT:
                        - Blazor's text input component
                        - @bind-Value (not @bind) for components
                        - Automatically validates and updates model
                        *@
                        <InputText class="form-input" @bind-Value="newPart.PartNumber" />
                    </div>
                    <div class="form-group">
                        <label class="form-label">Name *</label>
                        <InputText class="form-input" @bind-Value="newPart.Name" />
                    </div>
                    <div class="form-group">
                        <label class="form-label">Description</label>
                        @*
                        📖 InputTextArea:
                        - Multi-line text input
                        - rows="3" sets initial height
                        - Works same as InputText
                        *@
                        <InputTextArea class="form-input" @bind-Value="newPart.Description" rows="3" />
                    </div>
                    <div class="form-group">
                        <label class="form-label">Category</label>
                        @*
                        📖 InputSelect WITH @foreach:
                        - Dropdown populated from collection
                        - value="@cat.Id": Option value is category ID
                        - @cat.Name: Display text
                        - @bind-Value binds to CategoryId (nullable int?)
                        *@
                        <InputSelect class="form-select" @bind-Value="newPart.CategoryId">
                            <option value="">Select category...</option>
                            @foreach (var cat in categories)
                            {
                                <option value="@cat.Id">@cat.Name</option>
                            }
                        </InputSelect>
                    </div>
                    <div class="form-group">
                        <label class="form-label">Manufacturer</label>
                        <InputText class="form-input" @bind-Value="newPart.Manufacturer" />
                    </div>
                    <div class="form-group">
                        <label class="form-label">Quantity</label>
                        @*
                        📖 InputNumber:
                        - Numeric input with validation
                        - Automatically parses to int/decimal
                        - Rejects non-numeric input
                        *@
                        <InputNumber class="form-input" @bind-Value="newPart.Quantity" />
                    </div>
                    <div class="form-group">
                        <label class="form-label">Location</label>
                        <InputText class="form-input" @bind-Value="newPart.Location" />
                    </div>
                    <div class="modal-footer">
                        @*
                        📖 BUTTON TYPES IN FORMS:
                        - type="button": Regular button, won't submit form
                        - type="submit": Triggers form submission
                        - Without type: Defaults to submit!
                        *@
                        <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="CloseModals">Cancel</button>
                        <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Save Part</button>
                    </div>
                </EditForm>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
}

@*
=============================================================================
MODAL - Part Detail View
=============================================================================
📖 SELECTED ITEM PATTERN:
- selectedPart != null renders detail view
- Click on list item sets selectedPart
- Click close/delete clears selectedPart
*@
@if (selectedPart != null)
{
    <div class="modal-overlay" @onclick="CloseModals">
        <div class="modal" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
            <div class="modal-header">
                <h3>@selectedPart.Name</h3>
                <button class="btn-icon" @onclick="CloseModals">&times;</button>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">
                @*
                📖 TABLE RENDERING:
                - Standard HTML table
                - Razor expressions in cells
                - Null handling with ?.Name ?? "N/A"
                *@
                <table class="table">
                    <tr>
                        <th>Part Number</th>
                        <td>@selectedPart.PartNumber</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Category</th>
                        <td>@(selectedPart.Category?.Name ?? "N/A")</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Manufacturer</th>
                        <td>@(selectedPart.Manufacturer ?? "N/A")</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Quantity</th>
                        <td>
                            <span class="part-quantity @GetQuantityClass(selectedPart)">
                                @selectedPart.Quantity
                            </span>
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Location</th>
                        <td>@(selectedPart.Location ?? "N/A")</td>
                    </tr>
                    @*
                    📖 CONDITIONAL TABLE ROWS:
                    - @if inside table - only renders row if condition true
                    - Cleaner than showing "N/A" for optional fields
                    *@
                    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(selectedPart.Description))
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <th>Description</th>
                            <td>@selectedPart.Description</td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </table>

                <div class="mt-md">
                    <h4>Adjust Quantity</h4>
                    <div style="display: flex; gap: 8px; align-items: center;">
                        @*
                        📖 REGULAR HTML INPUT VS Blazor Components:
                        - <input @bind="x"> works for simple cases
                        - InputNumber provides validation
                        - Use HTML input when you don't need validation
                        *@
                        <input type="number" class="form-input" style="width: 100px;" @bind="quantityChange" />
                        @*
                        📖 LAMBDA WITH PARAMETERS:
                        - @onclick="() => AdjustQuantity(1)"
                        - Passes different values to same method
                        - Alternative: separate methods for each action
                        *@
                        <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="() => AdjustQuantity(1)">Add</button>
                        <button class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="() => AdjustQuantity(-1)">Remove</button>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-footer">
                <button class="btn btn-danger" @onclick="DeleteSelectedPart">Delete</button>
                <button class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="CloseModals">Close</button>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
}

@*
=============================================================================
CODE BLOCK - Component Logic
=============================================================================
📖 @code DIRECTIVE:
- Contains C# code for the component
- Fields become component state
- Methods become event handlers
- Lifecycle methods hook into component events

📖 COMPONENT STATE:
- Any field can be state
- UI re-renders when state changes
- Call StateHasChanged() to force re-render
- Usually automatic after event handlers

📖 COMMON LIFECYCLE METHODS:
- OnInitialized[Async]: Component created
- OnParametersSet[Async]: Parameters updated
- OnAfterRender[Async]: DOM rendered
- Dispose: Component destroyed

📚 Full lifecycle: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/blazor/components/lifecycle
=============================================================================
*@
@code {
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // COMPONENT STATE (Fields)
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 📖 These fields are the component's "state"
    // When they change, Blazor re-renders affected parts of the UI

    /// <summary>
    /// The list of parts to display.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 📖 IEnumerable<Part>?:
    /// - IEnumerable: Can iterate over it (foreach)
    /// - ?: Nullable - can be null before data loads
    /// - Could also use List<Part> for more features
    /// </remarks>
    private IEnumerable<Part>? parts;

    /// <summary>
    /// Available categories for the dropdown.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 📖 Array.Empty<Category>():
    /// - Returns a cached empty array (memory efficient)
    /// - Better than new Category[] { } or new List<Category>()
    /// - Prevents null reference when rendering dropdown
    /// </remarks>
    private IEnumerable<Category> categories = Array.Empty<Category>();

    /// <summary>
    /// Current search query text.
    /// </summary>
    private string searchQuery = "";

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether data is currently loading.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 📖 LOADING STATE PATTERN:
    /// 1. Set isLoading = true before async operation
    /// 2. Perform async operation
    /// 3. Set isLoading = false in finally block
    /// 4. UI reacts to isLoading changes
    /// </remarks>
    private bool isLoading = true;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the "Add Part" modal is visible.
    /// </summary>
    private bool showAddModal = false;

    /// <summary>
    /// The currently selected part for detail view.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 📖 NULLABLE FOR "NO SELECTION":
    /// - null = no part selected, modal hidden
    /// - Part instance = part selected, modal shown
    /// - Common pattern for optional UI state
    /// </remarks>
    private Part? selectedPart;

    /// <summary>
    /// Model object for the "Add Part" form.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 📖 FORM MODEL:
    /// - new Part() creates empty object
    /// - Form inputs bind to this object's properties
    /// - On submit, this object is sent to service
    /// - Reset with newPart = new Part()
    /// </remarks>
    private Part newPart = new();

    /// <summary>
    /// Amount to add/remove in quantity adjustment.
    /// </summary>
    private int quantityChange = 1;

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // LIFECYCLE METHODS
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------

    /// <summary>
    /// Called when the component is first initialized.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 📖 OnInitializedAsync:
    /// - One of Blazor's lifecycle methods
    /// - Called once when component is created
    /// - Perfect for loading initial data
    /// - Can be sync (OnInitialized) or async (OnInitializedAsync)
    ///
    /// 📖 protected override:
    /// - protected: Accessible to derived classes
    /// - override: Replaces the base class implementation
    /// - Must match base class signature
    ///
    /// 📖 LIFECYCLE ORDER:
    /// 1. Constructor
    /// 2. SetParametersAsync
    /// 3. OnInitialized[Async]
    /// 4. OnParametersSet[Async]
    /// 5. OnAfterRender[Async]
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        await LoadData();
    }

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // DATA LOADING
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------

    /// <summary>
    /// Loads parts and categories from the service.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 📖 TRY-FINALLY PATTERN:
    /// - try block: Code that might fail
    /// - finally block: Always runs, even if exception occurs
    /// - Ensures isLoading is set to false even on error
    /// - No catch block: Let errors propagate
    ///
    /// 📖 WHY NO CATCH BLOCK?
    /// - Blazor has built-in error handling
    /// - Let framework handle unexpected errors
    /// - Only catch what you can meaningfully handle
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task LoadData()
    {
        isLoading = true;
        try
        {
            // 📖 Parallel calls: These don't depend on each other
            // Could optimize with Task.WhenAll for parallel execution:
            //   var partsTask = PartsService.GetPartsAsync();
            //   var categoriesTask = PartsService.GetCategoriesAsync();
            //   await Task.WhenAll(partsTask, categoriesTask);
            //   parts = await partsTask;
            //   categories = await categoriesTask;
            parts = await PartsService.GetPartsAsync();
            categories = await PartsService.GetCategoriesAsync();
        }
        finally
        {
            isLoading = false;
            // 📖 StateHasChanged() is called automatically after event handlers
            // If called outside event handler, you'd need: await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        }
    }

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // EVENT HANDLERS
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------

    /// <summary>
    /// Handles keyup events in the search input.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 📖 KeyboardEventArgs:
    /// - Contains information about the keyboard event
    /// - e.Key: The key pressed ("Enter", "a", "Escape", etc.)
    /// - e.Code: Physical key code
    /// - e.CtrlKey, e.ShiftKey: Modifier keys
    ///
    /// 📖 DEBOUNCING (not implemented here):
    /// For better UX, consider debouncing search:
    /// - Wait 300ms after last keystroke before searching
    /// - Reduces API calls during fast typing
    /// - Use System.Threading.Timer or third-party library
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task OnSearchKeyUp(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(searchQuery))
        {
            parts = await PartsService.GetPartsAsync();
        }
        else
        {
            parts = await PartsService.SearchPartsAsync(searchQuery);
        }
        // 📖 UI automatically re-renders because we're in an event handler
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Shows the "Add Part" modal.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 📖 RESETTING FORM STATE:
    /// - new Part() clears all previous form data
    /// - Important when modal is reused
    /// - Alternative: Call Clear() method on existing object
    /// </remarks>
    private void ShowAddPartModal()
    {
        newPart = new Part();  // Fresh object for the form
        showAddModal = true;   // Show the modal
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Selects a part for detail view.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 📖 SETTING STATE SHOWS MODAL:
    /// - selectedPart != null triggers modal rendering
    /// - Simple pattern: state controls UI visibility
    /// </remarks>
    private void SelectPart(Part part)
    {
        selectedPart = part;
        quantityChange = 1;  // Reset quantity input
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Closes all modal dialogs.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 📖 SINGLE CLOSE METHOD:
    /// - Closes any modal that might be open
    /// - Simpler than separate methods per modal
    /// - Bound to overlay click and close buttons
    /// </remarks>
    private void CloseModals()
    {
        showAddModal = false;
        selectedPart = null;
    }

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // DATA OPERATIONS
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------

    /// <summary>
    /// Saves the new part to the database.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 📖 VALIDATION:
    /// - Simple check before calling service
    /// - Could use DataAnnotations for declarative validation:
    ///   [Required] public string PartNumber { get; set; }
    /// - EditForm + ValidationSummary shows validation errors
    ///
    /// 📖 REFRESH AFTER SAVE:
    /// - LoadData() fetches fresh data from database
    /// - Ensures list shows the new part
    /// - Alternative: Add to local list without reloading
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task SaveNewPart()
    {
        // Guard clause: Validate required fields
        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(newPart.PartNumber) || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(newPart.Name))
            return;  // Don't save if validation fails

        await PartsService.CreatePartAsync(newPart);
        await LoadData();   // Refresh the list
        CloseModals();      // Close the form
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Adjusts the quantity of the selected part.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="direction">1 for add, -1 for remove.</param>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 📖 DIRECTION PARAMETER:
    /// - Reuses same method for add/remove
    /// - quantityChange * direction gives actual change
    /// - Positive direction = add, negative = remove
    ///
    /// 📖 TERNARY OPERATOR:
    /// - direction > 0 ? In : Out
    /// - Concise way to select between two values
    /// - Equivalent to if/else but in an expression
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task AdjustQuantity(int direction)
    {
        if (selectedPart == null) return;  // Guard clause

        var change = quantityChange * direction;
        var type = direction > 0 ? TransactionType.In : TransactionType.Out;

        await PartsService.UpdateQuantityAsync(selectedPart.Id, change, type);

        // Refresh the selected part to show updated quantity
        selectedPart = await PartsService.GetPartByIdAsync(selectedPart.Id);

        // Refresh the list too (quantity shown in cards)
        await LoadData();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Deletes the currently selected part.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 📖 OPTIMISTIC UI (not implemented here):
    /// Alternative approach for better UX:
    /// 1. Remove from local list immediately
    /// 2. Close modal
    /// 3. Call delete API in background
    /// 4. On failure, re-add to list and show error
    ///
    /// Current approach is simpler but slower:
    /// 1. Call delete API
    /// 2. Reload entire list
    /// 3. Close modal
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task DeleteSelectedPart()
    {
        if (selectedPart == null) return;

        await PartsService.DeletePartAsync(selectedPart.Id);
        await LoadData();    // Refresh to remove deleted part
        CloseModals();       // Close detail modal
    }

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // HELPER METHODS
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------

    /// <summary>
    /// Returns CSS class name based on part's stock level.
    /// </summary>
    /// <returns>CSS class for styling quantity display.</returns>
    /// <remarks>
    /// 📖 COMPUTED CSS CLASSES:
    /// - Method returns string used as CSS class
    /// - Called in Razor: class="part-quantity @GetQuantityClass(part)"
    /// - Could also be a computed property if no parameters needed
    ///
    /// 📖 STYLING BY STATE:
    /// - Red for out of stock (quantity-out)
    /// - Yellow for low stock (quantity-low)
    /// - Green for OK (quantity-ok)
    /// - CSS definitions are in the stylesheet
    /// </remarks>
    private string GetQuantityClass(Part part)
    {
        if (part.Quantity <= 0) return "quantity-out";       // Out of stock
        if (part.Quantity <= part.MinQuantity) return "quantity-low";  // Low stock
        return "quantity-ok";  // Normal stock level
    }
}

@*
=============================================================================
📖 EXTENSION IDEAS FOR LEARNING
=============================================================================

1. ADD VALIDATION:
   Use DataAnnotations in your model:

   public class Part
   {
       [Required(ErrorMessage = "Part number is required")]
       [StringLength(50, MinimumLength = 3)]
       public string PartNumber { get; set; }
   }

   Then in your form:
   <EditForm Model="newPart" OnValidSubmit="SaveNewPart">
       <DataAnnotationsValidator />
       <ValidationSummary />
       ...
   </EditForm>

2. ADD DEBOUNCED SEARCH:
   using System.Timers;

   private System.Timers.Timer? _searchTimer;

   private void OnSearchInput(ChangeEventArgs e)
   {
       _searchTimer?.Stop();
       _searchTimer = new System.Timers.Timer(300);
       _searchTimer.Elapsed += async (s, e) =>
       {
           await InvokeAsync(async () => { await PerformSearch(); });
       };
       _searchTimer.Start();
   }

